  [PASS] K04a THE HEADLINE CHECK, AND IT CAN FAIL: the external field the measured zero-velocity radii demand must be the one the surrounding galaxies actually supply, to better than a factor 3.  A ratio far from 1 kills the completed law as surely as the isolated version's factor 2 killed the isolated one   (median demanded/supplied = 0.39 over 5 groups; individually Local Group: 0.12, M81 group: 0.21, Cen A group: 4.61, M83 group: 9.12, IC 342 group: 0.39)
  [PASS] K04b GROUP BY GROUP, not just in the median: the group that needs the biggest external field must be the group whose neighbours supply the biggest one.  With this few groups the correlation is weak evidence either way and the check records that   (r(log e_N demanded, log e_N supplied) = +0.268 over 5 groups -- the right sign)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
MUTATION CONTROLS AND SENSITIVITY
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Local Group: e_N with Virgo = 0.00934, without Virgo = 0.00510 (55% of it comes from the Local Volume itself)
  [PASS] M04-bug the concave-summation bug must be shown, not asserted: summing each source's own deep-MOND field instead of composing the summed NEWTONIAN field through nu must inflate the answer by a large factor   (the wrong composition inflates e_N by a median factor 5.6 over the five groups -- the first version of this script used it and its verdict is void)
  [PASS] M04a doubling Virgo's assumed baryonic mass must move the supplied field by less than sqrt(2), since the deep-MOND field goes as sqrt(M) -- if the answer swung more than that the result would be an assumption about Virgo   (doubling Virgo moves e_N by x1.325)
  [PASS] M04b shuffling which group's supplied field goes with which group's demand must NOT improve the agreement -- if a random pairing does as well, the agreement carries no group-level information   (true |median ratio - 1| = 0.612; shuffled median 0.531 (fraction of shuffles doing better: 0.803) -- with 5 groups this test has almost no power and that is the finding)
  a_0 x 4, isolated branch                       : +0.1498 dex  (predicted +0.1505 = log10(4)/4)
  a_0 x 4, EFE branch, e_N held fixed (WRONG)    : +0.0029 dex
  a_0 x 4, EFE branch, e_N propagated (CORRECT)  : +0.0503 dex
  [PASS] M04c with the external field switched on at the level the sky supplies, R_0 must still respond to a_0, or the completed law has no a_0 in it and fails criterion (2).  The mutation is propagated consistently: e_N goes as a_0^(-1/2) because the external source is itself deep-MOND   (a_0 x 4 moves R_0 by +0.0503 dex on the EFE branch against +0.1498 on the isolated one -- the EFE branch is 0.34x as sensitive, i.e. a_0 enters at power 0.084 instead of 0.250)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE UPSILON LEVER
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  M_b enters as M_b^(1/4) and the stellar share is ~0.86, so d log R_0/d log Upsilon_K = +0.215 on the prediction side.
  On the SUPPLY side, e_N is a deep-MOND sum over sqrt(M_b) of the neighbours, so d log e_N/d log Upsilon = +0.430.
  The test statistic is (demanded/supplied).  d log(demanded)/d log Upsilon comes from inverting R_0(e_N):
    d log R_0/d log e_N = -0.153 near the relevant field.  It is SMALL, and that is the whole
    problem: inverting a measured R_0 for e_N AMPLIFIES every error by 1/|slope| = 6.6.
  d log (demanded/supplied) / d log Upsilon_K = +0.979
  [FAIL] K04-UPS the Upsilon lever on the test statistic must be under 0.3 dex per dex   (+0.979 dex per dex)

========================================================================================================================
THE RESTATEMENT TEST
========================================================================================================================
  (K04) has three inputs: M_b, e_N, and a_0.  From v^4 = G M_b a_0 you can build v_flat and therefore the
  M_b^(1/4) factor -- that half closes, as k02 already recorded.  You cannot build the e_N dependence:
  the BTFR is a statement about an isolated system and has no external field in it at all.  Nor can you
  build H_Lambda.  So (K04) DOES NOT CLOSE as a restatement.  It is the first candidate in this slate that
  survives criterion (5) outright.  What it must now survive is criterion (3): the data.

========================================================================================================================
VERDICT -- k04
========================================================================================================================
  The isolated Lambda edge over-predicts by a median factor 2.03 (k02's liability, reproduced here).
  Curing it needs a median external field of e_N = 0.0045.
  The Local Volume plus Virgo supplies a median e_N = 0.0095.
  Ratio demanded/supplied = 0.39.
  Read it both ways.  If the ratio is near 1 the framework's Lambda edge is consistent once its own
  external-field effect is included, and the candidate is alive but UNDERPOWERED at five groups.  If the
  ratio is far from 1 the candidate is dead and k02's liability stands unrepaired.  Either way the
  external field is now measured rather than assumed, which is what k02 could not do.
  The honest limits: five groups; a scalar nu(y + e_N) prescription the repository's item 8 has already
  found optimistic; a deep-MOND vector sum for a field that a nonlinear theory does not add vectorially;
  a cold-baryon mass that misses warm gas; and Virgo's baryonic mass known to a factor ~2.

RESULT: 8 checks, 1 FAIL -> ['K04-UPS the Upsilon lever on the test statistic must be under 0.3 dex per dex']   rc=1
